"<class name=\"XrealCameraFeed\" inherits=\"CameraFeed\" xmlns:xsi=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance\" xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation=\"../class.xsd\">\n"
"<brief_description>The XREAL glasses&#39; RGB camera exposed as a Godot [code]CameraFeed[/code], in full colour, through the native C ABI.</brief_description>\n"
"<description>Add it to the [code]CameraServer[/code] and call [code]poll_frame()[/code] each frame to grab the latest frame, then sample [code]get_y_texture()[/code], the R8 luma, and [code]get_cbcr_texture()[/code], the RG8 chroma, in a YCbCr-to-RGB shader to display it; [code]addons/godot_xreal/shaders/xreal_ycbcr*.gdshader[/code] do exactly that. It is present only on glasses that carry an RGB camera, such as the One Pro but not the Air 2 Ultra, so gate on [code]XrealSystem.is_camera_supported()[/code].[br][br][b]Read frames through the custom getters, not [code]CameraTexture[/code].[/b] If you know Godot&#39;s [code]CameraFeed[/code] this is the surprise: frames do NOT flow through the standard [code]CameraServer[/code] texture route by default. On this build a [code]CameraTexture[/code] bound to a script-fed feed shows only the placeholder, so the addon bypasses it: [code]poll_frame()[/code] updates the two plain [code]ImageTexture[/code]s above and every consumer, whether the camera panel, blend capture, the stream or photo capture, samples those directly. Code that consumes feeds through the standard [code]CameraFeed[/code] API can opt in with [code]feed_camera_server[/code], which additionally pushes each frame into the base feed through [code]set_ycbcr_images[/code], at the cost of a second per-frame GPU upload.</description>\n"
"<methods>\n"
"<method name=\"poll_frame\">\n"
"  <return type=\"bool\" />\n"
"  \n"
"  <description>\n"
"  Poll the latest RGB-camera frame and refresh [code]get_y_texture()[/code] and [code]get_cbcr_texture()[/code], the textures the YCbCr-to-RGB shaders sample, which is the display route. With [code]feed_camera_server[/code] on it also pushes the frame into the base [code]CameraFeed[/code] as [b]separate Y and CbCr[/b] images ([code]FEED_YCBCR_SEP[/code]) for standard [code]CameraServer[/code] consumers. It returns [code]true[/code] when a frame was grabbed on this call. Call it once per frame from a driver.[br][br]The camera publishes slower than a 60 Hz render loop polls, so [code]false[/code] is the normal result on the calls in between: the grab is gated on the SDK frame timestamp and does no copy or texture upload unless the frame actually changed. The textures keep their last contents, so a [code]false[/code] return needs no handling by the caller.\n"
"  </description>\n"
"</method>\n"
"\n"
"<method name=\"get_y_texture\">\n"
"  <return type=\"Texture2D\" />\n"
"  \n"
"  <description>\n"
"  The luma (Y) plane as an [code]R8[/code] texture; sample [code].r[/code] for Y. It is [code]null[/code] until the first frame.[br][br]The concrete class depends on the active path: an [code]ImageTexture[/code] on the Image and GL-direct paths, a [code]Texture2DRD[/code] on the Vulkan [code]vk_rd[/code] path. Both are [code]Texture2D[/code], which is all a shader uniform needs, and the instance is stable for the life of the capture path, so holding the reference across frames keeps working.\n"
"  </description>\n"
"</method>\n"
"\n"
"<method name=\"get_y_data\">\n"
"  <return type=\"PackedByteArray\" />\n"
"  \n"
"  <description>\n"
"  The retained CPU copy of the luma plane, a dense 8-bit greyscale image ready to wrap as an OpenCV [code]CV_8UC1[/code] Mat with no conversion. It is empty unless [[code]Self::cpu_luma_step[/code]] is non-zero, and until the first frame after enabling it. Use [[code]Self::get_y_data_size[/code]] for its dimensions: they are the camera&#39;s divided by [code]cpu_luma_step[/code], not the texture&#39;s.[br][br]The returned array shares copy-on-write storage with the feed&#39;s, so this call is a refcount bump rather than a copy. Holding it across frames is safe: the next frame&#39;s write forks it instead of mutating the snapshot underneath a reader, which is what makes it safe to hand to a worker thread.\n"
"  </description>\n"
"</method>\n"
"\n"
"<method name=\"get_y_data_size\">\n"
"  <return type=\"Vector2i\" />\n"
"  \n"
"  <description>\n"
"  Dimensions of [[code]Self::get_y_data[/code]] in pixels, or [code](0, 0)[/code] when it is empty.\n"
"  </description>\n"
"</method>\n"
"\n"
"<method name=\"get_cbcr_texture\">\n"
"  <return type=\"Texture2D\" />\n"
"  \n"
"  <description>\n"
"  The chroma plane as an [code]RG8[/code] texture, with [code].r[/code] as Cb (U) and [code].g[/code] as Cr (V). It is [code]null[/code] until the first frame. See [[code]Self::get_y_texture[/code]] for the concrete class per path.\n"
"  </description>\n"
"</method>\n"
"</methods>\n"
"\n"
"<members><member name=\"feed_camera_server\" type=\"bool\" default=\"\">Also push each frame into the base [code]CameraFeed[/code] through [code]set_ycbcr_images[/code], the route standard [code]CameraServer[/code] consumers read. It is [b]off by default[/b], because nothing in the addon reads it, a [code]CameraTexture[/code] bound to this script-fed feed showing only the placeholder on this build, and it duplicates every frame&#39;s GPU upload. Turn it on only for external code that consumes [code]CameraServer[/code] feeds through the standard API.</member><member name=\"cpu_luma_step\" type=\"int\" default=\"\">Retain a CPU-readable copy of the [b]luma[/b] plane each frame, for [[code]Self::get_y_data[/code]], which is the path for OpenCV and friends, since the GPU-upload path deliberately keeps no CPU copy.[br][br][code]0[/code], the default, is off and makes no copy. [code]1[/code] is the full 1280x720. [code]2[/code] and [code]4[/code] take every 2nd or 4th pixel and row, giving 640x360 or 320x180. The luma plane is already a dense 8-bit greyscale image, so the result needs no conversion: it is directly a [code]CV_8UC1[/code] Mat.[br][br]The cost is one copy per grabbed frame, measured at [b]about 296 us at [code]step = 1[/code] and about 278 us at [code]step = 2[/code][/b] on the X4000. Those are 6% apart, not 4x apart: a strided read still touches every cache line of the source, so this is read-bound whatever the step. [b]Do not raise [code]step[/code] expecting to save copy time.[/b] Its value is entirely downstream, in the 4x fewer pixels the CV code then has to process.[br][br]Read the result from the [code]frame_changed[/code] signal rather than polling the getters; see [[code]Self::get_y_data[/code]] and [code]docs/plans/camera-feed-plan.md[/code].[br][br]Luma only: the chroma is not retained. Colour CV would need the other two planes and a [code]cvtColor[/code], and note that the plane order is [b]YV12[/b], not I420. Its 1-3 ms would dominate this copy entirely, so it stays unimplemented until something actually needs it.</member></members>\n"
"</class>\n"
